      "n_contexts": 12,
      "answer": "The context does not contain enough information to trace a complete regulatory path from the FAA to the maintenance crew servicing the CFM LEAP-1B engine.\n\nWhat the context does establish:\n\n- FAA issues airworthiness directives under 14 CFR Part 39 when an unsafe condition exists and is likely to exist or develop in other products of the same type design (\u00a739.5).\n- ADs are legally enforceable rules applying to aircraft, aircraft engines, propellers, and appliances (\u00a739.3), and operators who do not meet an applicable AD are in violation (\u00a739.7).\n- For the 737 MAX, FAA AD 2024-01-02 requires MCAS Software v2.0 installation within 90 days of its February 15, 2024 effective date, per Boeing Service Bulletin (revision 2, January 8, 2024).\n- The 737 MAX CMM (rev12) lists related ADs for LEAP-1B engine mount inspection, including FAA AD 2024-01-02 and FAA AD 2022-03-07, and specifies inspection intervals and AMM task references.\n\nHowever, the context does not describe an actual chain of delegation, work authorization, or task assignment from the FAA to a specific maintenance crew for the LEAP-1B engine, nor does it connect the AD requirements to the CMM inspection tasks as a formal regulatory path. Any such trace would require information not present in the retrieved documents.",
