      "retrieval_mode": "full_context",
      "routing_reason": "full_context_profile",
      "n_contexts": 12,
      "answer": "FAA AD 2024-01-02 (effective February 15, 2024) supersedes AD 2022-03-07 and requires, within 90 days after the effective date, installation of MCAS Software version 2.0 or later per Boeing Service Bulletin [SSN_REDACTED], Revision 2, dated January 8, 2024, plus an FCOM revision, an AOA sensor inspection within 500 flight hours, and compliance reporting within 30 days.\n\nBoeing's MCAS Software Version 2.0 release notes (Document D6-87700-100, dated January 8, 2024) state the software is mandatory and released in conjunction with FAA AD 2024-01-02, supersedes v1.7.3 and all earlier versions, and directs installation per Boeing Service Bulletin [SSN_REDACTED], Revision 2, dated January 8, 2024 (estimated 3.5 hours per aircraft).\n\nSouthwest's fleet registry (dated January 1, 2024) lists FAA AD 2024-01-02 as \"COMPLIANCE DUE\" with a deadline of May 15, 2024; all 87 737 MAX 8 aircraft had MCAS v1.7.3 installed, with updates scheduled February-May 2024 (phased, ~10 aircraft/month) to target MCAS v2.0 per Boeing SB [SSN_REDACTED], Rev 2. The registry also records FAA AD 2020-05-11 and FAA AD 2022-03-07 as compliant fleet-wide.\n\nNote: The context does not state that Southwest's updates were actually completed-only the planned schedule as of January 1, 2024.",
      "citations": [
        "FAA-AD-2020-05-11",
        "FAA-AD-2022-03-07",
        "FAA-AD-2024-01-02"
      ],
